    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Corbin Carroll** (74.18) pacing the lineup, both benefiting from highly favorable matchups against suspect arms. **Mickey Moniak** draws a +18% boost from Grant Holmes\u2019 metrics, and his dynamic platoon advantage remains our most efficient engine for upside. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** overcomes a slight park penalty to remain an elite start; his projected involvement in stealing bags against Blade Tidwell makes him a foundational piece despite Tidwell\u2019s respectable xERA. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants attention at 73.99, specifically due to his optimized interaction with Rhett Lowder\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a tangible lift to his already strong base projection.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical tightropes, most notably with **Isaac Paredes** (51.73) and **Jose Caballero** (69.06), both of whom carry weather risks with rain probabilities hovering around 36-39%. While **Jose Caballero** maintains a high score due to his platoon edge against Patrick Sandoval, the potential for a shortened game makes his speed-heavy profile volatile. On the bench, **Josh Naylor** (50.39) narrowly missed the cut. While he possesses a solid base projection, his matchup against a high-tier arm like Dylan Cease forced the optimizer to prioritize the platoon advantages offered by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.52) and the bottom-of-the-order dynamism of **Austin Riley** (63.42). \n\nOur primary concern remains the reliance on \"Lineup Pending\" designations for players like **Gabriel Moreno** and **Max Muncy**. If the official lineups shuffle **Max Muncy** further down the order, his utility score will erode quickly. We are opting to keep **Zach McKinstry** on the bench despite his favorable SB-conducive matchup, as the strength of Tarik Skubal as an opposing pitcher makes him a clear fade compared to the favorable xERA matchups we are attacking elsewhere in the field. **James Wood** remains sidelined on the IL, leaving us to rely on our active depth to maintain the volume required for our 5x5 Roto goals.",
